type: web_research
title: Claude Code hooks community patterns for destructive commands
source: "https://github.com/disler/claude-code-damage-control, https://github.com/27Bslash6/schlock, https://github.com/kenryu42/claude-code-safety-net, https://github.com/steeef/claude-hooks"
key_insights[9]: "permissionDecision 'ask' WORKS (prompt fires) but bug #17356: permissionDecisionReason is NOT shown to user in normal CLI view — only visible in debug mode (Ctrl+O)","Community consensus: disler/claude-code-damage-control uses tiered approach — hard deny for worst (rm -rf, DROP TABLE, terraform destroy), ask for moderate (git stash drop, git branch -D)","schlock (27Bslash6) uses bashlex AST parsing instead of regex — catches shell wrappers. Fail-safe: any exception → deny, not allow",kenryu42/claude-code-safety-net uses only deny (no ask). Env var SAFETY_NET_PARANOID_RM for extra rm protection,steeef/claude-hooks rm_check.py — smart gate that allows rm of git-ignored files but blocks others,"Official docs example uses deny for rm -rf, not ask","Deprecated top-level { decision: 'block' } still works as of v2.0.76 — maps to deny internally",Exit code 2 has bugs (doesn't block Write/Edit/Task). JSON on exit 0 is the reliable path,stdout must be pure JSON — shell profiles that print on startup corrupt parsing
anti_patterns[4]: Don't fail open on hook errors — community best practice is fail-safe (any exception → deny),Don't rely on permissionDecisionReason being visible to user for 'ask' decisions (bug #17356),Don't mix exit code 2 with JSON output — pick one mechanism per hook,Don't use regex alone for shell commands — can be evaded with bash -c wrappers (schlock uses bashlex)
recommendations[4]: "Use 'ask' for all rm commands — prompt fires even if reason isn't shown, user still sees exact command",Change error handling from fail-open (exit 0) to fail-safe (deny) following schlock/safety-net pattern,"For absolute-path rm -rf, keep PermissionRequest deny as hard backup in case PreToolUse fails",Consider deny over ask for rm -rf on absolute/home paths — forces Claude to explain to user first
